          </p>

          <p>
            We started from the self-propelled mowers we already rate highly in our <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">best self-propelled lawn mower</a> guide and cut the list to those with rear-wheel or all-wheel drive. Front-wheel-drive models did not make it, for reasons the next section explains. We then checked each remaining model against its manufacturer's published specifications for drive type, wheel size, torque, weight and cutting-height range, and dropped anything we could not verify.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also dropped mowers that are no longer sold new. The obvious casualty is Honda. The HRX217 and HRN216 were for years the default recommendation for hilly lawns, but Honda stopped producing gasoline walk-behind mowers for the US market in September 2023 and sold through remaining stock in 2024. A new HRX advertised today is old stock or a reseller listing, so check the warranty terms before buying one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick here is a walk-behind. That is deliberate. On a genuinely steep lawn, a walk-behind is the safer tool, because you can step away from it. Riding mowers and zero-turns have hard slope limits, which we cover in detail further down, and if most of your lawn sits inside those limits our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">best riding lawn mower</a> and <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">best zero-turn mower</a> guides are the better place to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we weighed the cost of each mower against how much of its extra capability a typical hilly lawn would use. All-wheel drive, bigger engines and bigger batteries all cost money, and on a gentle slope a good rear-wheel-drive mower does the job for less.
