        <div id="drive-systems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. RWD vs AWD vs FWD on a Slope
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important spec on a mower for hills is which wheels are driven. When you push any mower up a slope, its weight shifts toward the rear wheels. A rear-wheel-drive mower benefits from that: more weight on the driven wheels means more grip. A front-wheel-drive mower suffers from it: the front wheels unload, and on a steep enough climb they simply spin. The effect gets worse when the rear bag fills, because the bag adds weight behind the rear axle and lifts the front end further.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why front-wheel drive, which is common on budget self-propelled mowers, is the wrong choice for hills. It does have a real advantage on flat lawns with lots of obstacles, since you can tip the mower back onto its rear wheels to turn and the drive disengages. Our <a href="/front-wheel-vs-rear-wheel-drive-lawn-mower/">front-wheel vs rear-wheel drive lawn mower</a> guide covers that trade in detail, but on a slope the answer is simple: rear-wheel drive or better.
          </p>

          <p>
            All-wheel drive adds power to the front wheels as well. On a dry, moderate slope it is only a modest improvement over a good rear-wheel-drive mower, and independent testing has found the same. Where it earns its extra cost is on steep climbs, on damp grass and on side-slopes, where one driven wheel losing grip would stop a two-wheel-drive mower or let the deck slide downhill. With four wheels pulling, one slipping wheel is not the end of the pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            How the drive speed is controlled matters almost as much as which wheels it powers. A single-speed drive is tiring on a slope, because it pulls at the same pace uphill as on the flat and you end up either jogging or dragging. Variable systems such as Toro's Personal Pace, which reads how hard you push the handle, and EGO's Touch Drive, which puts a speed dial under your thumb, let you slow right down on a steep pitch without releasing the drive.
          </p>

