        <div id="slope-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Steep Is Too Steep?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every riding mower and zero-turn manual includes a maximum slope, and for most residential machines that figure is somewhere between 10 and 15 degrees. It is a limit, not a target. It assumes dry grass, good tires, a smooth surface and a skilled operator, and the manufacturers are clear that you should stay well under it when any of those conditions is missing. Walk-behind manuals are more varied, but they carry similar warnings about steep grades and about slopes near drop-offs, ditches and water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Degrees are hard to judge by eye, and most people badly underestimate their slopes. The easy way to measure is with a straight 4-foot board and a level. Rest one end on the slope, hold the board level, and measure the gap between the downhill end of the board and the ground. A gap of about 8.5 inches is roughly 10 degrees, and about 13 inches is roughly 15 degrees. A smartphone inclinometer app laid on a board works too.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">SLOPE CHEAT SHEET, 4-FOOT BOARD</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">about 5 degrees, easy for any mower</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8.5 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">about 10 degrees, typical zero-turn limit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">13 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">about 15 degrees, typical riding mower limit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">18 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">about 20 degrees, walk-behind only, with care</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Measure in several places, including the steepest part of the slope rather than the average. A lawn that is 8 degrees across most of its width can have a 16-degree band at the bottom where it meets a driveway or a ditch, and that band is where accidents happen. Also look at what is at the bottom. A moderate slope that ends at a pond, a retaining wall or a road is more dangerous than a steeper one that ends in more flat lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your slope is steeper than about 20 degrees, stop thinking about mowers. At that angle even a walk-behind is awkward to control and your footing becomes the problem. A <a href="/best-string-trimmer/">string trimmer</a> with a good harness, or replacing the grass with ground cover, is the safer long-term answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="push-vs-ride" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Across or Up and Down: Push vs Ride
          </h2>

          <p>
            The advice about mowing direction is opposite for walk-behind and riding mowers, and mixing them up is one of the most dangerous mistakes people make on a hilly lawn. The rule is simple: with a walk-behind mower, mow across the face of the slope. With a riding mower or zero-turn, mow up and down the slope, never across it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The walk-behind rule exists to protect your feet. If you mow up and down a slope with a push mower, you are either walking downhill with the mower in front of you, where it can run away, or walking uphill with the mower above you, where a slip can pull it back over your feet. Mowing across keeps you and the mower on the same level, so if you lose your footing you fall away from the deck rather than under it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Walk-behind: across the slope. Rider: up and down.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A walk-behind mowed up and down can roll back onto your feet. A rider driven across a slope can roll over sideways. The machines fail in different directions, so the safe mowing direction is different too.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The riding mower rule exists to prevent sideways rollovers. A rider has a high center of gravity, with the operator sitting on top of it, and driving across a slope puts all that weight toward the downhill wheels. A small bump or a hollow under the uphill wheel can tip it. Driving up and down keeps the weight between the wheels, and the risk becomes a loss of traction rather than a rollover, which is why manufacturers specify it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one refinement for walk-behinds. Turn at the end of each pass by swinging the mower uphill of you, not downhill, so that the deck is never below your feet. And if the slope is steep enough that you feel the mower pulling you sideways, it is too steep for that mower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rollover" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Rollover Risk on Riders and Zero-Turns
          </h2>

          <p>
            Riding mowers and zero-turns are responsible for the most serious mowing injuries, and slopes are behind a large share of them. The risk is not only sideways rollovers. On a steep downhill run the drive wheels can lose grip, and once they do, the machine slides regardless of what you do with the controls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Zero-turn mowers are particularly vulnerable to this. They steer and brake entirely through their rear drive wheels, and the front wheels are free-swiveling casters. On a slope where the rear tires lose traction, you lose steering and braking at the same moment, and the front casters offer nothing to correct with. That is why most zero-turn manufacturers set lower slope limits than lawn tractors, often around 10 degrees, and why our <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">best zero-turn mower</a> guide treats slopes as a reason to consider a different machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many zero-turns carry a rollover protection structure, a steel hoop behind the seat. It only works with the seatbelt on and the hoop raised, because it protects you by keeping you inside the space it creates. With the hoop folded down for a low branch, you should not be wearing the belt, and you should not be anywhere near a slope. Lawn tractors generally have no protection at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practical rules for riders on sloped lawns: never mow when the grass is wet, keep a full bag or a heavy attachment off the mower on grades, avoid sudden turns and stops on a slope, keep at least a mower's width away from ditch edges and water, and leave anything close to the manufacturer's limit to a walk-behind or a trimmer.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your property has a lot of ground over about 10 degrees, the honest recommendation is a walk-behind from this list for the slopes and a rider for the flat areas, not one machine for everything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wheels-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Rear Wheel Size, Tread and Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rear wheel size is the spec most buyers ignore and the one that makes the most difference on rough, sloping ground. A larger wheel rolls over a bump or hollow rather than dropping into it. On a slope, a wheel that drops into a hole tips the whole deck toward it, so the blade scalps the uphill side of the cut and the mower lurches. A 10- or 11-inch rear wheel smooths that out noticeably compared with the 7- or 8-inch wheels on cheaper mowers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the tread as well as the diameter. Deep, chevron-style tread grips slopes far better than the shallow, almost smooth tread on budget wheels, particularly in damp grass. Ball-bearing wheels roll more freely and wear more slowly under the side loads a slope puts on them, which matters because a worn wheel bushing lets the wheel wobble and the deck tilt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is a double-edged spec. A heavier mower presses its driven wheels into the ground harder and has more traction, which helps it climb. It is also harder to hold on line across a slope, harder to turn at the end of a pass and more tiring to push if the drive slips. On mixed lawns, the sweet spot is roughly 65 to 90 lb with rear-wheel or all-wheel drive doing the work.
          </p>

