            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Where the weight sits matters too. Battery mowers carry a heavy pack high on the deck, which raises the center of gravity and makes them feel less planted on a traverse than a gas mower of the same weight. And any mower with a full grass bag has a lot of weight hanging behind the rear axle, which is one of the best arguments for mulching on a slope. Our <a href="/best-mulching-mower/">best mulching mower</a> guide covers which decks do that well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery-vs-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Battery vs Gas on Grades
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery mowers have one clear advantage on a slope that rarely gets mentioned: they have no oil and no fuel. A walk-behind gas engine is typically splash-lubricated, and small-engine makers publish maximum operating angles for a reason. Run one across a steep face for long enough and oil can pool away from where it is needed, the engine can smoke, and fuel can weep from the cap vent. An electric motor does not care what angle it is held at.
          </p>

          <p>
            Their disadvantage is energy. On a hill, the drive motor works harder to push the mower uphill and the blade motor works harder to keep its speed in grass that is being cut at an angle. Both draw more current, so runtime falls. Manufacturer runtime ratings assume flat, dry lawns, and on a lawn that is mostly climbing you should plan on noticeably less. That is why the battery picks here come with large packs: the EGO LM2156SP with a 10.0Ah battery and the Greenworks Pro 80V with 5.0Ah at a higher voltage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Torque is the other variable. A strong gas engine keeps blade speed up in thick grass on a climb, and cheaper battery mowers can bog under the same load. The best current battery models have closed that gap. EGO rates the LM2156SP at 8.3 ft-lb and the LM2206SP at 11.1 ft-lb of torque, and in normal grass on a moderate slope neither struggles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our recommendation follows from that. If your lawn is under about half an acre and you mow it regularly, so the grass is never very tall, a good battery mower is the better tool for slopes. If your lawn is larger, often gets long between cuts or includes a lot of steep ground, gas still has the edge in stamina. Our <a href="/best-gas-lawn-mowers/">best gas lawn mowers</a> and <a href="/best-battery-powered-lawn-mower/">best battery-powered lawn mower</a> guides go deeper on each.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Mow a Hill Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mow slopes when the grass is dry. Wet grass is slippery for your feet and your wheels, and it clumps under the deck, which makes the mower heavier and the cut worse. Mid-afternoon on a dry day is ideal. If you must mow after rain, leave the slopes for last.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raise the cutting height on slopes. Slopes drain faster and dry out sooner than flat ground, so the grass on them is under more stress in summer, and a taller cut shades the soil and helps it hold moisture. Raising the deck also makes scalping less likely on crests and bumps, which is the most common cosmetic problem on a sloping lawn. For most cool-season grasses, 3.5 to 4 inches on slopes is a good target.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mulch rather than bag. A full grass bag adds significant weight behind the rear axle, which lightens the front wheels and, on a front-drive or all-wheel-drive mower, reduces traction just when you need it. Mulching returns the clippings to the lawn and keeps the mower's balance consistent through the whole pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the blade sharp. A dull blade tears grass rather than cutting it, and the torn tips brown and lose moisture faster, which a stressed slope can least afford. A dull blade also makes the engine or motor work harder, which on a battery mower means shorter runtime. Plan on sharpening at least once a season, or more often on sandy soil.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, wear shoes with real grip. Most slope injuries with a walk-behind mower begin with a slip, not a mechanical failure, and a proper boot with a lugged sole does more for your safety on a bank than any feature on the mower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Front-Wheel Drive for a Sloped Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Front-wheel drive is common on budget self-propelled mowers, and it is the wrong layout for hills. As you climb, weight shifts to the rear, the front wheels unload and spin, and a filling grass bag makes it worse. On any lawn with real slopes, pay the modest premium for rear-wheel or all-wheel drive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing Across a Slope on a Rider</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driving a riding mower or zero-turn across a slope puts its high center of gravity over the downhill wheels, where a bump under the uphill wheel can tip it. Ride up and down instead. With a walk-behind the rule reverses: mow across, so the mower can never roll back over your feet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Guessing the Grade Instead of Measuring It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most people underestimate their slopes, and a lawn that averages 8 degrees can have a 15-degree band where it meets a ditch or driveway. Use a 4-foot board and a level at the steepest spot. A gap of about 8.5 inches means roughly 10 degrees, the typical zero-turn limit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing Slopes When the Grass Is Wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet grass makes both your shoes and the mower's tires slip, and it clumps under the deck. On a slope that is how walk-behind users fall and how riders slide. Leave the slopes until the grass is dry, and if you mow after rain, do the flat ground first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hauling a Full Bag Up the Hill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full grass bag adds considerable weight behind the rear axle. That makes the mower harder to push uphill, lightens the front end and changes how it handles on a traverse. Mulching keeps the balance consistent, returns nutrients to the lawn and avoids the trip to empty the bag at the top.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best lawn mower for hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most hilly lawns, the Toro 22-inch Recycler All-Wheel Drive with Personal Pace is our top pick. All four wheels are driven, so it keeps climbing when one wheel slips on damp grass, and the drive matches your walking speed. For battery power, the EGO LM2156SP with rear-wheel drive and 8.3 ft-lb of torque is the best choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is rear-wheel or all-wheel drive better for hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rear-wheel drive handles most hilly lawns well, because weight shifts onto the rear wheels as you climb. All-wheel drive is better on steep climbs, side-slopes and damp grass, where one slipping wheel would stop a rear-drive mower. On dry, moderate slopes the difference is small, so pay for AWD only if your slopes are steep or often wet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you mow a hill up and down or side to side?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the mower. With a walk-behind, mow across the slope, side to side, so the mower cannot roll back onto your feet if you slip. With a riding mower or zero-turn, mow up and down, never across, because driving across a slope puts the weight over the downhill wheels and risks a sideways rollover.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How steep a slope can a riding mower handle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential riding mowers and zero-turns carry a manufacturer limit between 10 and 15 degrees, and many zero-turns sit at the low end. That limit assumes dry grass and a smooth surface. Stay well under it in practice, and use a walk-behind or string trimmer on anything close to the limit or near water or drop-offs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery lawn mowers good on hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Good ones are. Battery mowers have no oil or fuel, so operating angle does not harm them the way it can harm a splash-lubricated gas engine. The trade-off is runtime, which falls noticeably on a lawn that is mostly climbing. Choose a model with high torque and a large battery, such as the EGO LM2156SP or Greenworks Pro 80V.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a zero-turn mower good for hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Zero-turns steer and brake only through their rear wheels, with free-swiveling front casters, so if the rear tires lose grip on a slope you lose steering and braking together. Most manufacturers set limits around 10 degrees. For lawns with significant slopes, a walk-behind for the grades and a rider for the flat is safer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the safest way to mow a steep bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure it first. If it is over about 20 degrees, use a string trimmer or replace the grass with ground cover. Below that, use a rear- or all-wheel-drive walk-behind, mow across the slope when the grass is dry, wear boots with lugged soles, turn the mower uphill of you at the end of each pass, and raise the cutting height.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

